  "slug": "design-glue-eb6f",
  "summary": "Computational and structure-guided methods now design binders and glue degraders with defined selectivity, moving beyond empirical discovery.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is rational engineering of protein-protein interfaces and ligand-dependent modulation. Two preprints address molecular glue degraders: TrIP-MGRD targets transient PPI pockets, supported by a CDK12-DDB1 cryo-EM structure and the molecular glue C432 that stabilizes it, while an antibody-drug conjugate of a CCNK glue degrader achieves HER2-selective cyclin K degradation with antiproliferative effects in breast cancer. Computational design appears in a shared latent-space method for affinity-guided PDZ-binding peptides, including off-target-aware and dual-target design, and in MobGen, which de novo designs monobody binders against the SARS-CoV-2 Spike RBD. A paired deep mutational scanning study of full-length Lck maps activity and inhibitor sensitivity, enabling a nilotinib-sensitized variant. The new element is a shift from empirical discovery toward designed, selective, interface-aware modulation, although this pattern does not apply uniformly across all items.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-21"
}
